# Trivy suppressions for the FirmAware image.
#
# Every entry here is a HIGH or CRITICAL finding that trivy can see a fix for
# and that this project cannot take. Nothing else is suppressed: the Trivy step
# in .github/workflows/azure-ci.yaml still runs with exit-code 1 on HIGH and
# CRITICAL, and that gate is the compensating control for running a Basic
# container registry in dev, where ACR quarantine is unavailable. An entry that
# is not written down here fails the build.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why these cannot be fixed
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every entry descends from the two pins that make this image work on Azure ML:
# mlflow==2.22.5 in the `train` extra, and azureml-mlflow==1.62.0.post5 in the
# `azure` extra. In each case the version trivy wants is one the pin forbids,
# so a bump is not a trade-off between security and convenience; it is a set of
# requirements with no solution.
#
# mlflow==2.22.5 is a hard Azure ML constraint, not caution. AML's hosted
# tracking server implements the MLflow 2 REST surface and has no
# /api/2.0/mlflow/logged-models endpoint. MLflow 3's Model.log() calls
# _create_logged_model unconditionally -- no flag, no env var, no fallback --
# so every log_model against an AML workspace dies on a 404 *after* training
# has finished. Every mlflow fix version trivy names below is 3.x.
#
# What makes that acceptable rather than merely unavoidable is where the
# vulnerable code lives. With one exception noted below, the mlflow entries are
# all flaws in the *tracking server* and *model server*: the artifact HTTP
# handler, the `mlflow server` / `mlflow ui` Flask app, its authentication
# layer, and `mlflow models serve`. This image runs none of that. It uses
# MLflow purely as a client library -- mlflow.log_metric, mlflow.log_artifact,
# mlflow.pyfunc.load_model -- against the AML-hosted server. It has no
# entrypoint that starts an MLflow server, publishes no MLflow port, and the
# AML managed online endpoint serves through azureml-inference-server-http, not
# through MLflow. The reachable attack surface for these CVEs is not present.
#
# Reviewed against the deployed posture: the image runs as uid 10001, the
# workspace is behind private endpoints when network_isolation is on, and the
# only untrusted input the container handles is the scoring payload, which is
# schema-validated before any MLflow call.
#
# Re-check when azureml-mlflow declares a floor above mlflow 3, or when AML's
# tracking server grows /api/2.0/mlflow/logged-models. Either removes the pins
# and with them every line below.

# --- mlflow 2.22.5: tracking-server path traversal / arbitrary file read -----
# Reached through the server's artifact HTTP handlers. Not served here.
CVE-2025-15036
CVE-2025-15031
CVE-2026-2033
CVE-2026-2614

# --- mlflow 2.22.5: command / code execution in the server and model serving -
# Reached through `mlflow models serve` and the recipe/deployment entrypoints.
# This image serves via azureml-inference-server-http and never invokes them.
CVE-2025-15379
CVE-2026-0596
CVE-2026-2651
CVE-2026-4137
CVE-2025-14287

# --- mlflow 2.22.5: authentication and request forgery in the server ---------
# The default-password, auth-bypass, SSRF and DNS-rebinding issues are all in
# the Flask app's auth and proxy layers. Authentication to the tracking store
# here is Entra ID via azureml-mlflow; MLflow's own auth is never loaded.
CVE-2026-2635
CVE-2026-2652
CVE-2026-2393
CVE-2025-14279
CVE-2026-4035
# Same layer, same reason, added 2026-08-15. The trace API routes were never
# registered in mlflow.server.auth's _before_request validator map, so any
# authenticated user can read, modify or delete another experiment's traces.
# It is conditional on MLflow's own auth being enabled -- that is the module
# holding the missing check -- and this image never loads it. Tracking is the
# AML-hosted server, authorised by Entra ID through azureml-mlflow, which
# applies its own workspace RBAC and does not route through mlflow.server.
# The named fix, 3.14.0, is on the far side of the MLflow 2 pin above.
CVE-2026-8147

# --- mlflow 2.22.5: insecure temporary directory ------------------------------
# The one client-side item. It needs a local attacker already executing code
# inside the container to win a race in /tmp. The container is single-purpose,
# non-root, and runs one process; an attacker in a position to exploit this
# already has everything this would grant.
CVE-2025-10279

# --- pip's vendored copies, not installed packages ----------------------------
# msgpack 1.1.2 and setuptools 70.3.0 are never installed here. The real
# installed versions are msgpack 1.2.1 and setuptools 84.0.0, both already
# past these fixes. Trivy reports the old ones because pip ships
# pip/_vendor/bom.cdx.json, an SBOM of its own vendored tree, and trivy reads
# it as if it described the environment. They are identifiable in trivy's JSON
# because only these two carry a BOMRef and sit under the generic `Python`
# target rather than a .dist-info/METADATA path.
#
# They are also the only entries here that `ignore-unfixed` does not cover:
# trivy names a fix version for both, because it believes them to be real
# installs. No version bump can move them -- they are not dependencies this
# project resolves.
#
# The only way to clear them is to delete pip, which this image did for one
# release -- and that broke batch scoring, because AML's batch driver
# (driver/amlbi_main.py) runs `python -m pip` while initialising and exits 42
# before calling the scoring script when it is missing. The serving stage does
# not, which is why every pre-deployment check passed. See the Dockerfile.
#
# Nothing but pip imports pip/_vendor, and pip runs only when the AML driver
# invokes it, at container start, on input no user supplies.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
CVE-2025-47273

# Not a separate decision: mlflow 2.22.5 declares pyarrow<20, so the fix
# version trivy wants (23.0.1) is unreachable while the pin above stands. The
# flaw is a denial of service when parsing malformed Arrow IPC or Parquet
# input; this image reads only Parquet and CSV it wrote itself, from storage
# accounts with shared-key auth disabled.
CVE-2026-25087

# --- cryptography 48.0.1 -------------------------------------------------------
# The other pin: azureml-mlflow 1.62.0.post5 declares cryptography<49.0.0, and
# the two fixes are 49.0.0 and 50.0.0. Adding a floor produces
# ResolutionImpossible, not a patched image -- verified with pip.
#
# CVE-2026-69247 is a Bleichenbacher oracle in PKCS#7 EnvelopedData decryption
# (pkcs7_decrypt_der / _pem / _smime). Exploiting it requires an application
# that decrypts attacker-supplied PKCS#7 or S/MIME with its own private key and
# reflects the outcome. This project never calls those functions; it has no
# S/MIME or PKCS#7 path at all.
#
# CVE-2026-69249 is exponential backtracking in cryptography's own X.509 chain
# builder when a chain repeats a self-signed intermediate. That verifier is the
# `cryptography.x509.verification` API. Nothing here uses it: TLS chain
# validation on every outbound call goes through OpenSSL under requests and
# urllib3 against certifi's trust store, and the container makes no inbound TLS
# connections -- AML terminates TLS at the endpoint, ahead of the image.
CVE-2026-69247
CVE-2026-69249
